**Definition 3.1 (Bright D4 projectors and the comparison channel).**
Let $P=I-R_0$, $c_0=(e_2+e_5)/\sqrt2$, and let
$(f_1,f_2,f_3,f_4)=(e_N,e_3,e_{25},c_0)$ be an orthonormal basis
of the bright real space $P\mathbb R^5$. For $1\le i<j\le4$ and
$\epsilon\in\{1,-1\}$, put

$$
v_{ij,\epsilon}=\frac{f_i+\epsilon f_j}{\sqrt2},\qquad
Q_{ij,\epsilon}=v_{ij,\epsilon}v_{ij,\epsilon}^T.
\tag{3.1}
$$

Use these twelve preparation-equals-measurement atoms with weight
$1/3$, and the atom $a=b=d$ with weight one. The frame channel is

$$
\mathcal B(X)=R_0\operatorname{tr}(R_0X)
+\frac13\sum_{i<j,\epsilon}Q_{ij,\epsilon}XQ_{ij,\epsilon}.
\tag{3.2}
$$

The antipodal vectors underlying (3.1) are the classical $24$ roots
of $D_4$ on the real sphere $S^3$. Their real spherical $5$-design
property is literature-attested in
[Cohn–Conway–Elkies–Kumar, §4](https://arxiv.org/html/math/0607447v3).
That statement is an ingredient, not a complex projective-design
claim. The finite tensor and complex channel identities needed here
are derived in the following proof.

